# Licence notice of the imported as-is assets

T022, F141-FR-012, assumption CQ-030.

The scenarios of the fixture catalogue (`spec.md` §O.2) use as-is files of the two baselines:
the recorded input streams of the Claude and the prime stream formats, the two greeting projects,
the reverse sources and the learning events. Those files are imported into this directory as
**assets of the oracle**, byte for byte unchanged, and each one keeps the licence of the baseline
it came from. They are never modified here; a scenario that needs a changed file writes the change
in its own template or through a step of the scenario.

This repository is under the MIT licence (`LICENSE` at the root). The imported assets are not:
they stay under the licence of their baseline. The owner ruled this admissible because
`@specstride/oracle` is a package at layer `dev` that is `private` and is never shipped in the
product package (F141-FR-045, W-100-16), so no distributed artefact of the product carries them.

## One entry per baseline

The table below is the notice itself. `src/catalogue/fixture.ts` reads it
(`readAssetNotice`, `licenceOfBaseline`) and refuses a fixture whose asset names a baseline the
notice does not record, or declares a licence other than the one recorded here
(`assertAssetsCarryTheirNotice`).

| Baseline | Licence | Copyright holder |
|---|---|---|
| `specstride@9cab15d` | Apache-2.0 | The Specstride baseline authors |
| `mixture-of-loops@9c88f86` | Apache-2.0 | The mixture-of-loops baseline authors |

The baseline keys are the system and the short form of its pinned commit, exactly as the field
`baseline` of `schemas/fixture.schema.json` names them; the oracle derives them from
`reference/pins.json` (`assetBaselines`), so the notice and the pins cannot drift apart.

## Where an asset lies

An imported asset lies at `fixtures/assets/<baseline>/<path>`, where `<path>` is the path the
asset has inside its baseline. The baseline is part of the path, so the licence that applies to a
file is never in doubt.

The recorded input streams are imported under `fixtures/assets/streams/<baseline>/<path>` (T123):
one directory for the whole kind, the baseline inside it, and the as-is path under that, so the
rule above still decides the licence. They are the 18 input streams of the Claude format
(`lib/fixtures/stream-golden/claude/*.jsonl` of the Specstride baseline) and the 10 of the prime
format (`lib/fixtures/prime-v3/*.jsonl`), each the member of the pattern name of FX-26 that carries
its name, and each byte for byte as the baseline holds it at the pinned commit.

One further as-is file is imported under the same root, `lib/fixtures/stream_test_adapter.py` of
the Specstride baseline (T131): it is the stream adapter the baseline's own quickstart adds to
reach a ROUTED stream format, and the three `fallback-<reason>` scenarios of FX-26 copy it into
their private writable copy of the reference tree, because no shipped registry entry sets
`stream=` and the three fallback reasons are unreachable without one. It is byte for byte as the
baseline holds it at the pinned commit.

## What is NOT imported

The as-is assets of the registry entry of the baseline that the product does not carry are **not**
imported (`spec.md` §O.7; F141-FR-012; F141-FR-029). Its name is found at run time, as the entries
of the reference registry minus the entries with as-is behaviour that the product carries, and it
is written in no file of the oracle — this notice included. `assertAssetIsNotOfTheEntryNotCarried`
in `src/catalogue/fixture.ts` takes the name as an argument and refuses the asset.

